WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.080
A brushless drive usually splits the control loop between firmware and a

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.260
separate gate driver.

3
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:11.903
SKU-1 puts both on one die and keeps the loop in hardware.

4
00:00:12.023 --> 00:00:16.223
Commands arrive from the host, and the processor sets the loop up.

5
00:00:16.223 --> 00:00:21.143
But it never sits inside the loop, so firmware timing never decides

6
00:00:21.143 --> 00:00:22.631
when a gate switches.

7
00:00:22.671 --> 00:00:26.531
The torque command turns into three phase drive in fixed hardware,

8
00:00:26.531 --> 00:00:31.125
so the loop's latency is known, with a target under 1 microsecond.

9
00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:36.345
Seven PWM channels switch three half bridges, on a rail anywhere from

10
00:00:36.345 --> 00:00:38.565
5 to 120 volts.

11
00:00:39.645 --> 00:00:42.820
That voltage is why the die is BCD.

12
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.620
Where the rotor is, and how much current flows in each phase,

13
00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.020
both return straight to the regulator.

14
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.969
Firmware is never in that path.

15
00:00:53.009 --> 00:00:57.089
A thermal, overcurrent or overvoltage fault shuts the gates,

16
00:00:57.089 --> 00:01:01.278
and they stay latched until someone decides otherwise.

17
00:01:01.378 --> 00:01:03.858
SKU-1 is pre-silicon.

18
00:01:03.858 --> 00:01:06.258
Every figure is a target.

19
00:01:06.258 --> 00:01:11.098
The converter's isolation from switching edges, and the loop deadline under bus

20
00:01:11.098 --> 00:01:13.598
load, must still be proven

21
00:01:14.438 --> 00:01:16.778
Bring us your motor and your bridge.

22
00:01:16.778 --> 00:01:20.180
We will tell you what SKU-1 must prove.
